{"type": "nethackers_brief", "text": "# You're improving a NetHack bot\n\nYou are improving a Python program that plays **NetHack** through the **NetHack Learning Environment (NLE)**. Make **one** focused change that raises its score.\n\n## Vocabulary\n- **identity** \u2014 one character the bot plays: role-race-alignment-gender, e.g. `val-hum-law-fem`.\n- **seed** \u2014 a fixed game RNG. One seed pins *everything* random \u2014 dungeon layout, monster and item generation, every roll \u2014 so the same seed always plays out the identical game. Not just a map: one concrete, fully-determined playthrough.\n- **score** \u2014 BALROG progression: starts near 0 and rises as the bot survives, descends, and advances. Higher is better.\n- **overall** \u2014 the mean score across all identities (every identity's seeds pooled).\n- **focused change** \u2014 one coherent idea (a single hypothesis), not necessarily a small edit. \"Focused\" means the *idea* is singular, not that the diff is small: carrying it out may take a large change \u2014 a refactor, a drastic rewrite, whatever the idea needs. One idea, any amount of code.\n\n## Your goal\nYou are improving this bot as **val-dwa-law-fem**. Raise its score. It currently scores **0.410**.\n\n## Per-seed detail\nThe table above averages over each identity's seeds. **`/refs/parent-eval.json`** has one row per seed (15 = 1 identity \u00d7 15 seeds): the `trajectory_id`, its `character` (identity), the `progress` score, the deepest `milestone`, and the `cause_of_death`. Read it to see which dungeons this bot does worst on and how it dies there.\n\n## References\nYou also have a read-only **`/refs/`** folder. Start with **`/refs/CONTEXT.md`** \u2014 it lists what's there: a pristine copy of this bot (`parent/`), its per-seed results (`parent-eval.json`), and other changes that were tried with the score each reached (`attempts.md`, with their code under `attempts/`).\n\n## How to make your change\n1. The bot lives at **`/workspace`** \u2014 edit the strategy code in the `autoascend/` package, not the `arena_adapter.py` glue.\n2. Make **ONE** focused change, marked with a `# hypothesis: \u2026` comment saying what you expect it to improve.\n3. Keep the `make_agent()` \u2192 `reset()` / `act()` contract intact and make sure the code imports cleanly.\n4. **Don't game the score:** no branching on seed fingerprints, no exploiting scorer/NLE quirks \u2014 those don't generalize.\n\n## How to measure (exactly like the judge)\n```\npython -m nethackers.arena.run --solution /workspace \\\n  --batch '[[0,\"<identity>\"], \u2026]' --evaluation-id local --out /tmp/eval.json\n```\n- `--evaluation-id local` is the judge's seed namespace \u2014 any other id plays different, meaningless games.\n- Read per-seed results from the `--out` file.\n- **Run it as ONE foreground command and wait.** Give Bash a long timeout (up to 600000 ms) and evaluate a **small** sample of seeds so it finishes in that window. This sandbox is single-shot: do **not** background the eval (`run_in_background`), `sleep`-wait, or rely on task notifications \u2014 a backgrounded result is lost when your turn ends, and you'd choose your change blind.\n\n## Seeds\nYour training seeds are **0\u201314**. Prefer **general** NetHack improvements over tricks tuned to these particular dungeons \u2014 they won't hold up. You have live Python + NLE and the current bot as your starting point."}
