{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790510900101,"sessionID":"ses_f1d43fee7ffegxFfEwzzxm36mC","part":{"id":"prt_0e2c108a5001s4HF1Uw7yqP7Xf","messageID":"msg_0e2c1061c001tQaIbwyUsNSM5t","sessionID":"ses_f1d43fee7ffegxFfEwzzxm36mC","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I understand the potion-quaffing logic. The bot only quaffs potions in these situations:\n1. **Healing potions** (identified as 'healing', 'extra healing', 'full healing') when HP < 1/3 max or < 8 HP\n2. **Fruit juice** when fainting (Hunger.FAINTING)\n3. **Holy water** (blessed) in last resort\n4. **Last resort** when critically low HP, no safe prayer, carrying <4 capacity - quaffs unidentified potions as a gamble\n\nThe **contaminated potion death** at Dlvl 1 likely happened through path #4 (last resort) or possibly through accidental quaffing.\n\nNow, let me think about the fix. The key issue is that the bot doesn't generally identify unidentified potions before drinking them. It only drinks identified healing potions or uses last-resort gambits.\n\nMy focused change hypothesis: **Make the bot identify unidentified potions by reading them at altars, and only quaff identified potions (or known-safe potions like healing/fruit juice).**\n\nBut this is complex. Let me think of a simpler, more focused change.\n\nActually, let me re-read the problem statement once more:\n> Make **one** focused change, marked with a `# hypothesis: …` comment saying what you expect it to improve.\n\nThe change needs to be \"focused\" - one idea. It can be a large change, but it should be one coherent idea.\n\nLet me think about what single change would have the most impact for val-dwa-law-fem.\n\n**My top candidates:**\n\n1. **Potion identification**: Make the bot identify potions at altars before quaffing. This would prevent the contaminated potion death.\n\n2. **Elbereth engraving improvement**: Make the bot engrave Elbereth earlier and more reliably, which would prevent melee deaths like ogre and rothe.\n\n3. **Better early game combat selection**: Avoid fighting monsters when at low HP or without Elbereth.\n\n4. **Scroll of identify usage**: Make the bot use scrolls of identify to identify potions before drinking.\n\nLet me think about which is most impactful and most \"focused\" (single idea).\n\nActually, let me look at this from a different angle. What if I make a change that addresses BOTH the potion death AND the melee deaths? \n\n**Elbereth engraving** could help with melee deaths (ogre, rothe) but not with the potion death.\n\n**Potion identification** would help with the potion death but not directly with melee deaths.\n\nBut wait - if the bot identifies potions better, it might also get healing potions earlier and survive longer to reach higher experience levels, which would help with melee deaths too.\n\nLet me go with **potion identification** as my hypothesis. Here's my plan:\n\n**Change**: Make the bot identify unidentified potions by reading them at altars. The bot will:\n1. When it finds an altar, check for unidentified potions in inventory\n2. Drop unidentified potions on the altar and pray to identify them\n3. Only quaff potions that are identified as safe (healing, fruit juice) or are already identified\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Looking at the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy, it already drops unidentified items on the altar. But it doesn't pray - just dropping doesn't identify.\n\nWhat if I modify the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy to also pray after dropping? Or add a new step that prays at the altar?\n\nActually, in NetHack, you need to pray at the altar to identify items. Simply dropping them doesn't identify them. But the strategy currently just drops items.\n\nLet me think of an even simpler change. What if I just make the bot **pray at altars more frequently**, which would both identify items and provide other benefits?\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at what's really happening with the contaminated potion death. The bot has very few identified potions - it only drinks healing potions when critically low HP, fruit juice when fainting, and holy water in last resort. Most potions are unidentified and just sit in inventory.\n\nThe contaminated potion death likely happened because:\n1. The bot picked up a potion at Dlvl 1\n2. At some point, it drank it (perhaps via last resort when critically low HP, or by accident)\n3. It was a harmful potion (potion of sickness, etc.)\n\nThe simplest fix: **Make the bot avoid drinking unidentified potions unless they're identified as safe, and specifically, make it identify potions at altars.**\n\nLet me implement this. I'll modify the code to:\n1. Add identification of potions at altars\n2. Make the bot only quaff identified potions (or known-safe ones)\n\nActually, let me look at this from yet another angle. What's the simplest possible change that would fix the contaminated potion issue?\n\nWhat if I just make the bot **read unidentified potions at altars to identify them**? In NetHack, the `read` command (`r`) on an unidentified potion identifies it. The bot could do this.\n\nBut the bot would need to:\n1. Find an altar\n2. Go to it\n3. Read the potion\n4. Then quaff it if safe\n\nThis is a significant change. Let me think if there's a simpler approach.\n\n**What about just making the bot pray at altars more often?** Praying at altars:\n- Identifies items (including potions)\n- Can give intrinsics\n- Angers wrong-alignment gods\n- Provides gift messages (artifact generation)\n\nIf the bot prays at altars regularly, it would identify potions as a side effect, and also get other benefits.\n\nActually, looking at the code, the bot already has `identify_items_on_altar` which drops items on the altar. But it doesn't pray. What if I make it pray after dropping?\n\nBut wait - praying at a wrong-alignment altar angers the god. For a lawful Valkyrie, praying at a chaotic altar would anger the god. The bot needs to be careful about which altars it prays at.\n\nLet me look at the alignment handling. The bot is lawful, so it should pray at lawful altars.\n\nActually, let me take a completely different approach. What if the change is about **how the bot handles the early game** - specifically, engraving Elbereth immediately at the start and using it as the primary survival mechanism?\n\nLooking at the data:\n- 2/15 seeds die very early (0.037 and 0.051)\n- The rest survive to Dlvl 21-29\n\nIf I can make the early game more robust, the bot would consistently reach higher depths.\n\nLet me look at what the elite bot does differently. The comments mention \"the elite's early game is untouched until\" certain hazards. So the elite bot has some early-game optimizations.\n\nOK, I need to just make a decision and implement it. Let me go with **potion identification at altars** as my hypothesis. Here's my plan:\n\n**Hypothesis**: Making the bot identify unidentified potions by reading/praying at altars before quaffing will prevent contaminated potion deaths and raise the overall score from 0.410.\n\n**Implementation plan**:\n1. Modify or add a strategy that identifies potions at altars\n2. Ensure the bot only quaffs identified potions (or known-safe ones)\n3. Keep the make_agent() → reset() / act() contract intact\n\nActually, let me reconsider once more. The contaminated potion death happened at Dlvl 1. The bot is a dwarf Valkyrie. Dwarves have poison resistance intrinsic. But the potion death might not be from poisoning - it could be from any harmful effect.\n\nLet me look at what potions are at Dlvl 1 and what could kill the bot:\n- Potion of sickness - makes you sick, can lead to death\n- Potion of blindness - blinds you\n- Potion of hallucination - halluncinates\n- Potion of confusion - confuses you\n- Potion of speed - speeds you up (generally good)\n- Potion of gain ability - raises an attribute\n- Potion of extra healing - heals fully\n- Potion of healing - heals partially\n- Potion of water - creates water\n- Potion of drain ability - lowers an attribute\n- Potion of levitation - makes you levitate\n- Potion of paralysis - paralyzes you\n- Potion of enlightenment - gives experience\n- Potion of blindness - blinds you (duplicate)\n- Potion of see invisible - makes you see invisible\n- Potion of extra healing - heals fully\n\nIf the bot drank a potion of sickness or potion of drain ability at Dlvl 1, that could cause death.\n\nOK, let me just go ahead and implement potion identification. I'll make the bot read unidentified potions at altars to identify them, and only quaff identified safe potions.\n\nWait, actually I just realized something. Let me re-read the `identify_items_on_altar` code:\n\n```python\nyield any((item.status == Item.UNKNOWN for item in flatten_items(self.agent.inventory.items)\n           if item.can_be_dropped_from_inventory()))\n```\n\nThis yields True if there are unknown items that can be dropped. Then it drops them. But dropping unidentified items on an altar doesn't identify them in NetHack - you need to pray.\n\nWhat if I modify the strategy to pray after dropping? Let me check what happens when you pray at an altar in NetHack...\n\nActually, let me just implement a clean solution. I'll add potion identification logic.\n\nHere's my focused change:\n\n**Change**: Add a new strategy `identify_potions_at_altar` that:\n1. Finds an altar on the current level\n2. Drops all unidentified potions on the altar\n3. Prays at the altar to identify them\n4. After identification, only quaffs identified safe potions\n\nBut I need to be careful about praying at wrong-alignment altars. For a lawful character, praying at a chaotic altar would anger the god.\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. What's the simplest change that would prevent the contaminated potion death?\n\n**Simplest change**: Make the bot never quaff an unidentified potion. Instead, it should identify them first.\n\nBut how does the bot identify them? The existing `identify_items_on_altar` drops items but doesn't identify them. I need to add prayer.\n\nLet me look at how prayer works in the code.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just go ahead and implement the change. I'll add a method that identifies potions by praying at lawful altars, and integrate it into the bot's strategy.\n\nWait, I just had another idea. What if the change is much simpler - just make the bot **engrave Elbereth at the start of every game**? This would provide immediate protection against melee attacks, which would help with the ogre/rothe deaths. And for the potion death, Elbereth doesn't directly help, but surviving longer would give more time for identification.\n\nBut engraving Elbereth at start might not be enough. Let me think...\n\nActually, you know what, let me look at this from the perspective of what's actually missing in the code. The bot has:\n\n1. `identify_items_on_altar` - drops unknown items on altar (doesn't identify)\n2. Various combat and survival strategies\n3. Elbereth rest in the dive phase\n\nWhat's missing is a mechanism to **identify potions by reading them**. In NetHack, you can identify a potion by reading it (the `read` command). If the bot reads an unidentified potion at an altar, it gets identified.\n\nLet me implement this. Here's my plan:\n\n1. Add a new strategy or modify existing code to identify potions at altars\n2. The bot will: go to altar → drop unidentified potions → pray → identify potions\n3. After identification, the bot will only quaff identified safe potions\n\nActually, I realize I should look at how the bot currently handles altars and prayers. Let me check the agent's prayer code.\n\nOK, I've been thinking about this for too long. Let me just implement a change and see if it works. My focused change will be:\n\n**Make the bot identify unidentified potions at altars by praying after dropping them, and only quaff identified potions.**\n\nThis is one coherent idea (potion identification at altars), and it should address the contaminated potion death. It's also a general NetHack improvement that would help any identity.\n\nLet me implement this now.\n\nActually, wait. I want to make sure I understand the code well enough. Let me look at how the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy is called and how I can modify it.\n\nLooking at `global_logic.py:388-413`, the `identify_items_on_altar` is a `@Strategy.wrap` decorated method. It's called as a strategy in the preempt chain.\n\nThe strategy:\n1. Checks for altar on current level\n2. Checks BFS distance to altar\n3. Yields True if there are unknown droppable items\n4. Goes to altar\n5. Drops unknown items\n\nAfter dropping, it doesn't pray. I need to add prayer after dropping.\n\nBut actually, I realize that the `identify_items_on_altar` is used in the preempt chain in `exploration_strategy` and `tour_strategy`. I need to make sure my change doesn't break the existing flow.\n\nLet me think about the minimal change. What if I just add a `pray` step after dropping the items in `identify_items_on_altar`? \n\nIn NetHack, praying at your alignment altar is safe and identifies items. Praying at a wrong alignment altar angers the god. For a lawful Valkyrie, she should pray at lawful altars.\n\nThe bot already tracks alignment (`self.agent.character.alignment`), so I can check that.\n\nActually, let me look at a simpler approach. What if I just make the bot **pray at altars it encounters during exploration**, which would identify potions as a side effect? The bot already moves around the level, and if it passes by or goes to altars, it could pray.\n\nBut the bot doesn't currently pray proactively. Let me check...\n\nLooking at the agent.py prayer code (lines 2362-2371), the bot prays when:\n1. Safe to pray and low HP\n2. Fainting prayer due\n3. Threat prayer due\n4. Not prayer failed and weak with safe prayer gap and not eating before praying\n\nSo the bot already has prayer conditions, but they're mainly HP/hunger-related. Adding general altar praying would be a new behavior.\n\nOK, let me just go ahead and implement my change. I'll add potion identification at altars as a new strategy, and integrate it into the bot's exploration.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the entire approach. Maybe I'm overthinking this.\n\nLet me re-read the user's instruction:\n> Make **one** focused change that raises its score.\n\nAnd:\n> It currently scores **0.410**.\n\nThe user also provides references including `/refs/parent-eval.json` which shows per-seed results. The user says:\n> Read it to see which dungeons this bot does worst on and how it dies there.\n\nSo the key data is:\n- 3 seeds die very early (0.037-0.051)\n- Most seeds score 0.39-0.47\n- 2 seeds score 0.55-0.65\n\nThe \"focused change\" should address the early deaths. Let me think about what's the single most impactful change.\n\nLooking at the 3 early deaths:\n1. `killed by a contaminated potion` at Dlvl 1 (0.0369) - potion issue\n2. `killed by an ogre` at Xp:7 (0.0508) - melee issue  \n3. `killed by a rothe` at Xp:7 (0.0508) - melee issue\n\nFor #1 (potion): Identify potions before drinking\nFor #2 and #3 (melee): Better Elbereth usage or combat avoidance\n\nIf I had to pick ONE change that addresses all three, it would be... hmm, that's hard. They're different categories.\n\nBut actually, if the bot identifies potions better (fix #1), it survives the early game better and reaches higher XL, which would naturally help with #2 and #3 too (higher XL = more HP and combat prowess).\n\nSimilarly, if the bot uses Elbereth better (fix #2/3), it survives melee encounters better, which also gives more time for potion identification.\n\nSo either fix would help across the board. Let me pick **potion identification** as my hypothesis, since the contaminated potion death is the single worst outcome (0.0369 vs 0.051 for the melee deaths).\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The two Xp:7 deaths (ogre and rothe) both have 0.0508, which is slightly better than the 0.0369 potion death. But they're all very low.\n\nWhat if I make a change that helps both? **Better early game Elbereth engraving** would help with melee deaths, and **better potion identification** would help with the potion death.\n\nBut I can only do ONE change. Let me pick the one with the highest expected impact.\n\nGiven that:\n- 1/15 deaths are from contaminated potion (0.0369)\n- 2/15 deaths are from ogre/rothe at Xp:7 (0.0508 each)\n- The remaining 12/15 deaths are at higher depths (0.39-0.65)\n\nIf I fix the potion death, I gain 0.0369 in progress from that seed. But the seed might still die to something else later, so the actual gain might be less.\n\nIf I fix the ogre/rothe deaths, I gain 0.0508 from each of 2 seeds, but again, they might die to something else.\n\nActually, the overall score is a mean across all seeds. Currently 0.410. If 2 seeds improve from 0.051 to, say, 0.30 (a reasonable improvement but not ascension), the new mean would be:\n(0.4258 + 0.4664 + 0.30 + 0.30 + 0.5068*2 + 0.6016 + 0.5544*2 + 0.3929 + 0.4664 + 0.6465 + 0.5068 + 0.4258 + 0.5544) / 15\n\nActually, this is getting complicated. Let me just implement a solid change and see.\n\nLet me go with **potion identification at altars**. Here's my implementation plan:\n\n1. Modify the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy in `global_logic.py` to also pray at the altar after dropping unidentified items, which will identify them\n2. Ensure the bot only prays at altars of its own alignment (or unaligned) to avoid angering the god\n3. After identification, the bot will have identified potions and can quaff safe ones\n\nActually, wait. I just realized there might be a much simpler change. Let me look at the `identify_items_on_altar` code again:\n\n```python\nyield any((item.status == Item.UNKNOWN for item in flatten_items(self.agent.inventory.items)\n           if item.can_be_dropped_from_inventory()))\n```\n\nThis just checks if there are unknown items. Then it drops them. But dropping unidentified items on an altar doesn't identify them in NetHack.\n\nHowever, what if I just add a `pray` step after dropping? Let me check if the agent has a pray method...\n\nActually, the agent already has `self.pray()` method based on the code I saw earlier (line 2370: `self.pray()`).\n\nLet me look at the pray method.","time":{"start":1790510696613,"end":1790510900080}}}
