{"type":"reasoning","timestamp":1790510926376,"sessionID":"ses_f1d43fee7ffegxFfEwzzxm36mC","part":{"id":"prt_0e2c43abb0011OiMF4bE7sVDk2","messageID":"msg_0e2c43642001x5tlc7DWLuwZif","sessionID":"ses_f1d43fee7ffegxFfEwzzxm36mC","type":"reasoning","text":"Now I have a good understanding of the code. Let me now plan my focused change.\n\nMy hypothesis: **Making the bot identify unidentified potions at altars by praying after dropping them will prevent contaminated potion deaths and raise the overall score.**\n\nMy plan:\n1. Modify the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy in `global_logic.py` to pray at the altar after dropping unidentified items\n2. Add alignment checking to ensure the bot only prays at compatible altars (lawful bot at lawful altar, etc.)\n3. This will identify the dropped potions (and other items), preventing the bot from later drinking contaminated potions\n\nWait, but I need to think about this more carefully. The `identify_items_on_altar` strategy is called during exploration. If I add prayer to it, it might have side effects:\n- Praying at a wrong-alignment altar angers the god\n- The prayer timeout is reset\n- Might get \"Use my gift wisely\" message (artifact generation)\n\nFor a lawful Valkyrie, praying at a lawful altar is safe and identifies items. Praying at a chaotic altar would anger the god.\n\nThe bot needs to check the altar's alignment before praying. The altar alignment is tracked in `self.agent.current_level().altars`.\n\nLet me think about the implementation more carefully.\n\nActually, I just realized there might be an even simpler approach. What if I just make the bot **not drink unidentified potions at all**, and instead always drop them or keep them for later identification? The bot currently only quaffs identified healing/fruit juice potions anyway. If I just add a check to never quaff unidentified potions, the contaminated potion death wouldn't happen because the bot would never drink an unidentified potion.\n\nBut this could be harmful if the bot needs to drink a healing potion and doesn't have one identified. However, the bot already has identified healing potions when it needs them (from the HP < 1/3 check). The issue is specifically with the last-resort gambit that quaffs unidentified potions.\n\nLooking at the code again at line 2378-2414, the last resort quaffing of unidentified potions happens when:\n- `jf_config.LAST_RESORT` is True\n- `_critically_low_hp()` is True\n- `blstats.carrying_capacity < 4`\n- Not poly_buffer\n\nSo the bot only quaffs unidentified potions in last resort when critically low on HP and overloaded. This is the likely path for the contaminated potion death.\n\nWhat if I just remove or modify this last-resort potion quaffing? That would prevent the contaminated potion death, but might cause other issues (the bot might die from starvation instead of gambling on potions).\n\nActually, let me look at this from a different angle. What if I just make the bot **identify potions at altars before the last resort gambit**? That way, the bot would have identified potions and could quaff known-safe ones.\n\nOK, I've been going back and forth too much. Let me just implement a concrete change.\n\nMy final decision: **I will modify the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy to also pray at the altar after dropping unidentified items. This will identify the items (including potions), preventing later contaminated potion deaths.**\n\nHere's the specific change:\n1. In `global_logic.py`, modify `identify_items_on_altar` to pray at the altar after dropping items\n2. Check the altar's alignment - only pray at altars compatible with the bot's alignment\n3. Lawful bots pray at lawful altars, chaotic at chaotic, unaligned anywhere\n\nWait, but there's a subtlety. The `identify_items_on_altar` strategy might not always be called, and the bot might not encounter altars frequently enough. Let me think about whether this change alone would be sufficient.\n\nActually, looking at the data more carefully, the contaminated potion death happened at Dlvl 1, turn 11035. By that point, the bot has had many opportunities to find and identify potions at altars. The issue is that the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy drops items but doesn't identify them.\n\nLet me implement the change. I'll add prayer to the `identify_items_on_altar` strategy.\n\nBut wait - I need to be careful. The `identify_items_on_altar` is used in the preempt chain, and adding prayer might change the behavior in ways that could cause other issues (e.g., prayer failure, god anger).\n\nLet me think of an alternative implementation. What if I add a separate strategy or modify the agent's behavior to identify potions at altars, without modifying the existing `identify_items_on_altar` strategy?\n\nActually, let me just go ahead and implement the change. I'll modify `identify_items_on_altar` to pray after dropping. If the altar is the bot's alignment, it's safe. If not, I'll skip the prayer.\n\nHere's my implementation:\n\n```python\n@utils.debug_log('identify_items_on_altar')\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef identify_items_on_altar(self):\n    mask = utils.isin(self.agent.current_level().objects, G.ALTAR)\n    if not mask.any():\n        yield False\n\n    dis = self.agent.bfs()\n    mask &= dis != -1\n    if not mask.any():\n        yield False\n\n    yield any((item.status == Item.UNKNOWN for item in flatten_items(self.agent.inventory.items)\n               if item.can_be_dropped_from_inventory()))\n\n    (ty, tx), *_ = zip(*(mask & (dis == dis[mask].min())).nonzero())\n    self.agent.go_to(ty, tx)\n    items_to_drop = [item for item in flatten_items(self.agent.inventory.items)\n                     if item.can_be_dropped_from_inventory() and item.status == Item.UNKNOWN]\n    if not items_to_drop:\n        raise AgentPanic('items to drop on altar vanished')\n\n    # TODO: move chunking to inventory.drop\n    items_to_drop = items_to_drop[:self.agent.inventory.items.free_slots()]\n\n    self.agent.inventory.drop(items_to_drop)\n    \n    # Pray at the altar to identify the dropped items\n    # Only pray at altars compatible with our alignment\n   altar_align = self.agent.current_level().altars.get((ty, tx))\n    if altar_align is not None:\n        # Lawful: lawful altars; Chaotic: chaotic altars; Unaligned: any altar\n        bot_align = self.agent.character.alignment\n        pray_safe = (\n            (bot_align == Character.LAWFUL and altar_align == Character.LAWFUL) or \\\n            (bot_align == Character.CHAOTIC and altar_align == Character.CHAOTIC) or \\\n            bot_align == Character.UNALIGNED\n        )\n        if pray_safe:\n            with self.agent.atom_operation():\n                self.agent.step(A.Command.PRAY)\n```\n\nActually, wait. I need to check what `altar_align` values are. Looking at the character.py code, alignments are integers: CHAOTIC=0, NEUTRAL=1, LAWFUL=2, UNALIGNED=3.\n\nAnd the altar alignment is stored in `level.altars[pos]`, which is also an alignment integer.\n\nLet me verify this by checking the code more carefully.\n\nActually, let me look at how altar alignment is set. In `exploration_logic.py:272-287`:\n\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef check_altar(self):\n    level = self.agent.current_level()\n    pos = (self.agent.blstats.y, self.agent.blstats.x)\n    if pos in level.altars and level.altars[pos] == Character.UNKNOWN:\n        yield True\n        with self.agent.atom_operation():\n            self.agent.step(A.Command.LOOK)\n            r = re.search(r'There is an altar to [a-zA-Z- ]+ \\(([a-z]+)\\) here.', self.agent.message or self.agent.popup[0])\n            assert r is not None, (self.agent.message, self.agent.popup)\n            alignment = r.groups()[0]\n            assert alignment in Character.name_to_alignment, (alignment, self.agent.message)\n            alignment = Character.name_to_alignment[alignment]\n            level.altars[pos] = alignment\n            return\n\n    yield False\n```\n\nSo the altar alignment is identified by looking at the altar, and it's stored as an integer from `Character.name_to_alignment`. Initially it's `Character.UNKNOWN = -1`.\n\nOK, so I can check the altar alignment against the bot's alignment. For a lawful bot:\n- Lawful altar: safe to pray, identifies items\n- Chaotic altar: would anger the god, NOT safe\n- Neutral altar: might be safe or angering, depends\n- Unaligned altar: safe\n\nActually, in NetHack, the alignment rules for prayer are:\n- Praying at your alignment altar: god is pleased, items identified\n- Praying at opposed alignment altar: god is angered, bad things happen\n- Praying at neutral altar: neutral gods are pleased, others might be angered\n\nFor a lawful character:\n- Lawful altar: pleased\n- Chaotic altar: angered\n- Neutral altar: pleased if the god is neutral, otherwise might be angered\n- Unaligned altar: always safe (no alignment to anger)\n\nActually, I'm not sure about the exact NetHack rules. Let me just be conservative and only pray at altars that are the bot's alignment or unaligned.\n\nActually, for simplicity, let me just check if the altar alignment is UNKNOWN or the same as the bot's alignment. If it's UNKNOWN, we identify it first by looking. If it's the same alignment, pray. If it's opposed, skip.\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify.\n\n**My revised implementation**: I'll add a simple check - if the altar's alignment is UNKNOWN or matches the bot's alignment, pray. Otherwise, skip prayer. This should be safe and identify potions when possible.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just implement the simplest possible change and see what happens. I'll add prayer after dropping items in `identify_items_on_altar`, with basic alignment checking.\n\nLet me implement this now.","time":{"start":1790510906043,"end":1790510926369}}}
