n `global_strategy()`.\n\nLooking at `global_strategy()`, the last preempt before the fight strategies is `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy()`. I could add my engraving strategy before that, or I could modify the `tour_strategy()` to engrave at the start.\n\nLet me try modifying the `tour_strategy()` to engrave Elbereth at the start. I'll add the engraving right after `yield True`.\n\nBut I need to be careful. The engrave command requires the prompt \"What do you want to write in the dust here?\" and the bot needs to have a free hand and not be polymorphed.\n\nLet me implement this. I'll modify `tour_strategy()` to engrave Elbereth after the initial yield.\n\nActually, wait. Looking at the strategy structure more carefully, the `yield True` in `tour_strategy()` just means \"the condition is met, proceed.\" The actual engraving would need to happen as an action, not just as a condition.\n\nLet me rethink this. The strategy pattern in this codebase is:\n- A strategy is a function returning a generator\n- The generator yields exactly once at the start (the condition)\n- After the first yield, actions are performed\n- The generator should not yield actions before the first yield\n\nLooking at the pseudocode in the strategy docstring:\n```python\nStrategy.wrap\ndef brutal_fight_strategy(agent, max_distance):\n    # check condition\n    y, x = find_closest_monster()\n    if y == -1:  # no monster on the map\n        yield False\n    if max(abs(y - agent.blstats.x), abs(x - agent.blstats.x)) > max_distance:\n        yield False\n\n    yield True\n    # execute action\n    agent.go_to(y, x, stop_one_before=True)\n    agent.fight(y, x)\n```\n\nSo the pattern is:\n1. First yield: condition check (if False, strategy doesn't enter)\n2. Second yield: must be True (strategy enters)\n3. Then the generator does actions until StopIteration\n\nFor `tour_strategy()`, the first `yield True` means \"the condition is met, proceed.\" Then the while loop does the actual work.\n\nI think the best approach is to add the Elbereth engraving as a separate strategy that preempts the tour strategy, or to add it at the beginning of `tour_strategy()`.\n\nActually, let me look at how the strategies are composed. In `current_strategy()`:\n```python\nreturn self.tour_strategy().until(self.agent, self.dive.should_dive).before(self.dive.strategy())\n```\n\nThe `until` and `before` methods chain strategies. The `until` runs the strategy until a condition is met, then switches to another strategy. The `before` runs one strategy first, then the other.\n\nIf I want to engrave Elbereth at the very start, I should add it as a preempt strategy in `global_strategy()`, or modify `tour_strategy()` to engrave at the start.\n\nLet me try modifying `tour_strategy()` to engrave Elbereth right after the initial `yield True`. Here's my plan:\n\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef tour_strategy(self):\n    yield True\n    # Engrave Elbereth at the start for early-game survival\n    if self.agent.can_engrave():\n        self.agent.engrave('elbereth')\n    while 1:\n        ...\n```\n\nBut wait, the `engrave` method is an agent method, not a strategy yield. I need to either:\n1. Call `self.agent.engrave('elbereth')` directly (but this might not work within the strategy framework)\n2. Yield the engrave action through the strategy framework\n\nLooking at how other actions are done in strategies, they use `yield` to perform actions. For example, `agent.go_to(y, x)` is called within the strategy.\n\nBut the `engrave` method in the agent is more complex - it generates a sequence of actions (press '-', wait for prompt, write text, press '\\r'). \n\nLet me look at how the `elbereth_rest` strategy in `dive_logic.py` handles engraving. It might have a pattern I can follow.\n\nActually, let me look at a simpler approach. What if I just add the engraving command directly in the agent's main loop, before the strategies are run? Or what if I add it as the first action in the `tour_strategy()`?\n\nLet me look at how the `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy` works - it yields actions:\n\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy(self):\n    yielded = False\n    while (\n            self.agent.character.prop.blind or\n            self.agent.character.prop.confusion or\n            self.agent.character.prop.stun or\n            self.agent.character.prop.hallu or\n            self.agent.character.prop.polymorph):\n        if not yielded:\n            yield True\n            yielded = True\n\n        self.agent.direction('.')\n```\n\nSo strategies can yield actions (like `self.agent.direction('.')`). The strategy framework handles these yields.\n\nFor engraving, I need to yield the engrave action sequence. Let me look at how to do this.\n\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. Instead of modifying the strategy, let me add the Elbereth engraving in the agent's `main()` method, right after `init_finished = True` and before the main loop. This way, the engraving happens on the very first turns of the game, before any strategies are run.\n\nBut wait, the main loop at line 2754 `while 1:` already processes strategies. The strategies are run through the `global_strategy()` preempt chain. If I add engraving before the main loop, it would need to be part of the strategy chain.\n\nActually, looking at the main loop more carefully, the strategies are run inside the `while 1:` loop. The `autoascend_agent.Agent.main()` method is called from the driver, and it runs the strategy preempt chain.\n\nLet me look at how strategies are actually executed in the main loop.","time":{"start":1790511732733,"end":1790511743775}}}
