. `self.step_count += 1`\n5. `self._hb_actions[...] += 1`\n6. `self.score += reward`\n7. `self.update(observation, additional_action_iterator=gen())` - passes the generator to update\n\nThen in `update()`:\n```python\nif additional_action_iterator is not None:\n    is_next_action = True\n    try:\n        next_action = next(additional_action_iterator)\n    except StopIteration:\n        is_next_action = False\n\n    if is_next_action:\n        self.step(next_action, additional_action_iterator)\n        return\n```\n\nSo if the generator hasn't exhausted:\n1. It gets the next action from the generator\n2. Calls `self.step(next_action, additional_action_iterator)` recursively\n3. Returns\n\nIf the generator is exhausted:\n1. `is_next_action = False`\n2. Continues with normal update\n\nSo the engrave generator yields actions that are sent to the game after the ENGRAVE command. The generator I saw earlier (lines 1415-1432) yields:\n- `-` (to select the engrave command)\n- `n` (if \"Do you want to add to the current engraving?\")\n- spaces (while reading)\n- text characters (the engraving text)\n- `\\r` (to submit)\n\nOK, now I understand the full picture. To engrave Elbereth from within a strategy, I can call `self.agent.engrave('elbereth')`, which will:\n1. Send the ENGRAVE command to the game\n2. Generate the prompt \"What do you want to write with?\"\n3. Wait for the player to press '-' \n4. Then yield the text 'elbereth\\r'\n5. The engraving is done\n\nBut wait, the `engrave` method is designed to be called from the agent's normal flow, not from within a strategy's `run()` method. Let me check if calling it from a strategy would work.\n\nLooking at the `run()` method:\n```python\ndef run(self, return_condition=False):\n    gen = self.strategy()\n    if not next(gen):\n        ...\n    try:\n        next(gen)\n        assert 0\n    except StopIteration as e:\n        ...\n```\n\nThe strategy generator yields values. The first `next(gen)` gets the first yield (condition). The second `next(gen)` runs the strategy body. If the body calls `self.agent.engrave('elbereth')`, that method would:\n1. Create its own sub-generator\n2. Call `self.step(A.Command.ENGRAVE, sub_gen)`\n3. This sends the ENGRAVE command and sub-actions to the game\n\nBut there's a problem: the `step` method might fail or the engrave might not work correctly within the strategy context. Also, the strategy's `next(gen)` would need to complete (reach StopIteration) for the strategy to be considered \"done\" for that turn.\n\nActually, looking at the main loop:\n```python\nwhile 1:\n    ...\n    self.global_logic.global_strategy().run()\n    assert 0\n```\n\n`run()` is called every turn. If the strategy's generator doesn't complete in one call to `run()`, it would be restarted the next turn. But looking at `run()`:\n\n```python\ndef run(self, return_condition=False):\n    gen = self.strategy()\n    if not next(gen):\n        ...\n    try:\n        next(gen)\n        assert 0\n    except StopIteration as e:\n        ...\n```\n\nThe second `next(gen)` should raise StopIteration, which is caught. If it doesn't (the `assert 0` fires), that would be a problem. So the strategy generator MUST complete (raise StopIteration) within the second `next(gen)` call.\n\nThis means the strategy body (between the first and second `next(gen)` calls) must not have any long-running operations. It should yield/perform actions and then end.\n\nBut the `engrave` method calls `self.step()` which sends actions to the game and waits for responses. This could take multiple turns. So calling `engrave` from within a strategy's `run()` might not work because the strategy would need to complete in one turn.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think of a simpler approach.\n\nActually, wait. Looking at the main loop more carefully:\n\n```python\nwhile 1:\n    ...\n    try:\n        self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n        self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n        self.on_panic()\n    finally:\n        last_step = self.step_count\n\n    self.global_logic.global_strategy().run()\n    assert 0\n```\n\nThe `run()` is called every turn. But the strategy generator is RE-CREATED every turn ( `gen = self.strategy()` ). So the strategy starts fresh each turn. The `next(gen)` calls advance the generator, and when it raises StopIteration, the strategy is \"done\" for that turn. But the next call to `run()` creates a new generator from `self.strategy()`, so the strategy starts from the beginning again.\n\nWait, that can't be right. If the strategy starts from the beginning each turn, then `next(gen)` would always get the first yield (the condition). The second `next(gen)` would also get the first yield again (since the generator was just created). But that doesn't make sense because the strategies have while loops and state.\n\nLet me re-read `run()`:\n```python\ndef run(self, return_condition=False):\n    gen = self.strategy()\n    if not next(gen):\n        if return_condition:\n            return False\n        return None\n    try:\n        next(gen)\n        assert 0\n    except StopIteration as e:\n        if return_condition:\n            return True\n        return e.value\n```\n\nOK so:\n1. `gen = self.strategy()` creates a new generator\n2. `next(gen)` advances to the first yield. If the first yield is False, return None.\n3. `next(gen)` advances again. If the generator raises StopIteration, catch it and return e.value.\n4. If the second `next(gen)` does NOT raise StopIteration, `assert 0` fires.\n\nSo the second `next(gen)` is expected to either:\n- Raise StopIteration (generator ends after the first yield)\n- Fire assert 0 (generator has more yields than expected)\n\nBut the strategies have while loops! How does this work?\n\nLet me look at `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy`:\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy(self):\n    yielded = False\n    while (\n            self.agent.character.prop.blind or\n            ...):\n        if not yielded:\n            yield True\n            yielded = True\n\n        self.agent.direction('.')\n```\n\nThis strategy has a while loop that continues as long as the condition is true. The first `yield True` is got by the first `next(gen)`. Then the second `next(gen)` would enter the while loop and... what happens?\n\nIf the while condition is still true, the generator would hit `self.agent.direction('.')` and then... the while loop would continue, and the next `next(gen)` call would hit the `if not yielded:` check again, but `yielded` is now True, so it would skip to `self.agent.direction('.')`. But there's no more yield after that! The generator would end (StopIteration).\n\nWait, but `self.agent.direction('.')` is called as a side effect, not as a yield. So after calling `direction('.')`, the generator would have no more yields and would raise StopIteration.\n\nBut that means the strategy only takes ONE turn per call to `run()`. The next call to `run()` creates a new generator, and the whole process repeats. The `yielded` variable is local to the generator, so it's reset each turn.\n\nOH WAIT. I think I finally understand. The strategy generator is created fresh each turn (`gen = self.strategy()`). So each turn, the generator starts from the beginning. The first `next(gen)` gets the first yield. The second `next(gen)` runs the body until the next yield or StopIteration.\n\nFor `wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy`:\n- First `next(gen)`: yields True (enters the strategy)\n- Second `next(gen)`: enters the while loop, `yielded` is False, so yields True and sets `yielded = True`, then calls `self.agent.direction('.')`. After `direction('.')`, the generator has no more yields, so StopIteration is raised.\n\nWait, but `yield True` is a yield statement. After yielding True, the generator pauses. The `next(gen)` call resumes the generator at the yield point. After the yield, the generator continues with `yielded = True`, then `self.agent.direction('.')`, and then ends (StopIteration).\n\nBut the `next(gen)` is called TWICE in `run()`:\n1. First `next(gen)`: Gets the first yield (True). The generator is now paused after the yield.\n2. Second `next(gen)`: Resumes the generator from the yield point. It hits `if not yielded:` - but `yielded` was set to True by... wait, no. `yielded` is a local variable in the generator. The first `next(gen)` gets the yield True, but does it set `yielded = True`? No! The `yielded = True` is on the second iteration of the while loop, which happens AFTER the first yield.\n\nHmm, I'm getting confused again. Let me just trace through the code literally.\n\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef wait_out_unexpected_state_strategy(self):\n    yielded = False\n    while (\n            self.agent.character.prop.blind or\n            ...):\n        if not yielded:\n            yield True\n            yielded = True\n\n        self.agent.direction('.')\n```\n\nGenerator source code (what `self.strategy()` returns):\n```\nLine 1: (function body starts)\nLine 2: yielded = False\nLine 3: while (condition):\nLine 4:     if not yielded:\nLine 5:         yield True       <-- first yield\nLine 6:         yielded = True\nLine 7:     self.agent.direction('.')\nLine 8: (end of function, StopIteration)\n```\n\nWhen `run()` is called:\n1. `gen = self.strategy()` - creates the generator object\n2. `next(gen)` - resumes the generator from the start\n   - Executes `yielded = False`\n   - Executes `while (condition):` - True\n   - Executes `if not yielded:` - True (yielded is False)\n   - Executes `yield True` - pauses the generator, returns True\n   - Generator state: after the yield, yielded = True (but this line hasn't executed yet!)\n\nWait, no. When a generator hits `yield True`, it pauses and returns True. The code after the yield (`yielded = True`) has NOT been executed yet. The generator resumes from where it left off when `next(gen)` is called again.\n\n3. `next(gen)` - resumes the generator from the yield point\n   - Executes `yielded = True` (finally! the line after the yield)\n   - Checks `while (condition):` - True (still blind/confused, etc.)\n   - Executes `if not yielded:` - False (yielded is now True)\n   - Skips the if block\n   - Executes `self.agent.direction('.')` - side effect\n   - End of while loop body\n   - Goes back to while condition check\n   - If condition still True, loops back to `if not yielded:` - False, skips\n   - Executes `self.agent.direction('.')` again\n   - ... this continues until the condition becomes False\n   - Eventually, the while loop exits\n   - Function ends, StopIteration is raised\n\nOK so the second `next(gen)` call runs the ENTIRE while loop body until StopIteration. This means the strategy takes effect for ONE call to `run()`, and then the generator is done.\n\nBut wait, the main loop calls `run()` every turn. And `run()` creates a NEW generator each time (`gen = self.strategy()`). So each turn, the strategy starts from the beginning. The `yielded` variable is reset each turn.\n\nThis means:\n- Each turn, `run()` creates a fresh strategy generator\n- The first `next(gen)` gets the first yield (condition)\n- The second `next(gen)` runs the strategy body until StopIteration\n- The strategy effects (like `direction('.')`) happen for one turn only\n- Next turn, a new strategy generator is created, effects repeat\n\nOK, so now I understand the pattern. Each strategy run takes effect for exactly one turn (the turn on which `run()` is called). The next turn, a new strategy instance is created.\n\nGiven this, if I want to engrave Elbereth at the start of the game, I need to add the engraving to the strategy that runs on the first few turns.\n\nNow, the question is: which strategy runs first? The `global_strategy()` returns a chain of preempt strategies. The first strategy in the chain is `current_strategy()`, which is `tour_strategy().until(...).before(...)`.\n\nLooking at `tour_strategy()`:\n```python\n@Strategy.wrap\ndef tour_strategy(self):\n    yield True\n    while 1:\n        ...\n```\n\nThe first `yield True` means \"the condition is met, proceed.\" The second `next(gen)` in `run()` would enter the while loop and run until StopIteration.\n\nIf I add engraving right after `yield True` in `tour_strategy()`, it would engrave Elbereth on the first turn that `run()` is called. But `run()` is called from the main loop, which starts after `init_finished = True` and `jf_scenario.apply(self)`.\n\nLooking at the main loop flow:\n1. `init_finished = True` after the initial setup (ESC x2, parse character, autopickup)\n2. `jf_scenario.apply(self)` - applies dev scenario state\n3. Enter the `while 1:` main loop\n4. Each turn: `self.global_logic.global_strategy().run()`\n\nSo the first call to `run()` happens on the first turn of the main loop, after the initial setup. This is turn 1 (or turn 3 considering the ESC x2 at the start).\n\nIf I add `self.agent.engrave('elbereth')` right after `yield True` in `tour_strategy()`, it would engrave on the very first turn of the main loop. But I need to make sure the conditions are right:\n- Bot must be able to engrave (not polymorphed, not hands welded, not at forbidden position)\n- The engrave prompt must be available\n- No monsters adjacent (or Elbereth won't help)\n\nActually, looking at `can_engrave()`:\n```python\ndef can_engrave(self):\n    if self.character.prop.polymorph:\n        return False\n    if self.hands_welded():\n        return False\n    return (self.blstats.y, self.blstats.x) != self._forbidden_engrave_position\n```\n\nAnd the `engrave` method handles the prompt sequence. If I call `self.agent.engrave('elbereth')` and the conditions aren't right, it will just ESC out (not engrave).\n\nLet me try adding the engraving to `tour_strategy()`. I'll modify it to engrave Elbereth after the initial yield, if conditions allow.\n\nActually, I realize there might be a simpler approach. Instead of modifying the strategy, I could add the engraving in the agent's `main()` method, right after `init_finished = True` and before the main while loop. But the strategies are run inside the main loop, so I can't add actions before the strategy chain starts.\n\nWait, actually I can. Looking at the main loop:\n```python\nwhile 1:\n    inactivity_counter += 1\n    ...\n    if inactivity_counter >= 5 or self._pass_turn_after_error:\n        ...\n    \n    try:\n        self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n        self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n        self.on_panic()\n    finally:\n        last_step = self.step_count\n\n    self.global_logic.global_strategy().run()\n    assert 0\n```\n\nThe first thing in the main loop is `self.step(A.Command.ESC)` x2, then `run()`. I can't easily add engraving before `run()` because the strategies need to be the first things that act.\n\nActually, wait. I could add the engraving as a preempt strategy in `global_strategy()`, before the tour strategy. Looking at `global_strategy()`:\n\n```python\ndef global_strategy(self):\n    return (\n        self.current_strategy().repeat()\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.dive.hunt_strategy(),\n            ...\n        ])\n        .preempt(self.agent, [\n            self.solve_sokoban_strategy()...\n        ])\n        ...\n    )\n```\n\nThe `.preempt()` method adds strategies that can interrupt the main strategy. The preempt strategies are checked first, and if they don't act, the main strategy runs.\n\nI could add an Elbereth engraving strategy as the first preempt, so it runs before the tour strategy. But I need to make sure it only runs at the start (first few turns) and then yields to the tour strategy.\n\nActually, let me try the simplest approach: modify `tour_strategy()` to engrave Elbereth at the start. I'll add it right after `yield True`.\n\nBut I need to be careful about the strategy pattern. After `yield True`, the second `next(gen)` in `run()` will execute the strategy body. If I add `self.agent.engrave('elbereth')`, it will call the engrave method, which sends actions to the game. The strategy body would then end (StopIteration), and the engraving would happen over multiple turns as the strategy is re-created each turn.\n\nWait, but the strategy is re-created each turn. So if I engrave on turn 1, the engraving action starts on turn 1, and the prompt sequence continues on subsequent turns. But the strategy generator is re-created each turn, so the engraving would need to be re-initiated each turn until it completes.\n\nThis is getting really complicated. Let me try a different approach entirely.\n\n**New approach: Add Elbereth engraving in the `main()` method, before the strategy chain.**\n\nLooking at the main method:\n```python\ndef main(self):\n    try:\n        init_finished = False\n        try:\n            with self.atom_operation():\n                self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n                self.step(A.Command.ESC)\n                ...\n            init_finished = True\n        except BaseException as e:\n            self.handle_exception(e)\n\n        assert init_finished\n        jf_scenario.apply(self)\n\n        last_step = self.step_count\n        inactivity_counter = 0\n        forced_turns = 0\n        turn_after_forced = None\n        while 1:\n            ...\n            self.global_logic.global_strategy().run()\n            assert 0\n    except AgentFinished:\n        pass\n```\n\nI could add the Elbereth engraving right after `jf_scenario.apply(self)` and before the main while loop. But the main while loop is `while 1:`, and the first thing inside is `inactivity_counter += 1`. I'd need to add the engraving before the while loop starts.\n\nBut wait, the `run()` call is inside the while loop. If I add engraving before the while loop, it would happen once before the strategies start. But the strategies are what drive the game forward. If I engrave before the strategies, the engraving would happen on the first few turns before the strategy chain takes over.\n\nActually, let me re-read the main method flow:\n\n1. Initial setup (ESC x2, parse, enhance view, autopickup) - done in the try block before the while loop\n2. `jf_scenario.apply(self)` - applies dev scenario state\n3. `assert init_finished`\n4. Main while loop starts\n5. Inside the while loop: ESC x2, on_panic, `run()`\n\nSo the initial setup (step A.Command.ESC x2) happens before the while loop. I could add an engrave step after the initial setup but before `jf_scenario.apply(self)` and the main loop.\n\nBut actually, looking at the code flow, the `step(A.Command.ESC) x2` is part of the initial setup, and then `jf_scenario.apply(self)` applies the scenario state. After that, the main loop starts with `run()` every turn.\n\nI think the cleanest approach is to modify `tour_strategy()` to engrave Elbereth at the start. Let me try this.\n\nActually, you know what, let me just try a simple test. I'll add the engraving call to `tour_strategy()` and see if it works. If the engrave prompt isn't available, the `engrave` method will just ESC out, which is harmless.\n\nLet me modify `tour_strategy()` to add Elbereth engraving after the initial `yield True`.","time":{"start":1790511813336,"end":1790511937637}}}
